B.FG=new A.V(21,"Their block builds your line",B.a1,"A threat the opponent must answer is worth more than a threat they can ignore, because a forced answer is a move you get to plan around. The strongest version of the idea: make them fill the square directly underneath a square you want.",B.aA,B.Lo,3,null,B.l,"The row was one square too high to play, so you made them build the step up to it. That is the whole trick: when a square you want is unreachable, threaten the square below it. Their only defence puts your piece within reach.",!0,B.IG,6,"You have three amber sunburst pieces side by side on the second row, in columns 4, 5 and 6. The square that finishes them is column 7 on the second row \u2014 and it is out of reach, because column 7 is empty and a piece there would land on the floor. Column 4 does not touch that. It builds a diagonal: your new piece, then your pieces in columns 5 and 6, sloping down to the floor of column 7. Now they must play column 7, and it is the only move they have.")
